    "totalStudyCount": 3,
    "evidenceByGoal": "Weight management/body weight; safety, tolerability, pharmacokinetics, and pharmacodynamics. The eligible record was a randomised, placebo-controlled, multiple-ascending-dose phase 1b trial at a single US centre in otherwise healthy adults aged 18–55 years with BMI 27.0–39.9 kg/m². [21] Ninety-six participants were randomised; 95 were exposed and included in the safety and full-analysis datasets. [21] Participants received once-weekly subcutaneous cagrilintide 0.16, 0.30, 0.60, 1.2, 2.4, or 4.5 mg with once-weekly subcutaneous semaglutide 2.4 mg; doses were co-escalated over 16 weeks, followed by 4 weeks at the target dose and 5 weeks of follow-up. [21] The comparator was matched placebo with semaglutide 2.4 mg, with 3:1 randomisation within cohorts. [21] At week 20, mean body-weight reductions were 15.7% with cagrilintide 1.2 mg, 17.1% with 2.4 mg, and 15.4% with 4.5 mg, versus 9.8%, 9.8%, and 8.0% with the corresponding pooled or matched placebo cohorts; the reported estimated treatment differences were −6.0%, −7.4%, and −7.4%, respectively. [21] Glycaemic parameters improved in all treatment groups independently of cagrilintide dose, and hormone changes were similar across treatment groups. [21] Pharmacokinetic findings were dose-proportional for cagrilintide, and cagrilintide did not affect semaglutide exposure or elimination. [21] Of 566 adverse events reported in 92 participants, 207 (37%) were gastrointestinal disorders; most adverse events were mild to moderate, and the proportion with one or more adverse event was similar across treatment groups. [21] Key limitation: the source concludes that future larger and longer trials are needed to fully assess efficacy and safety. [21] The findings do not verify cagrilintide monotherapy efficacy because the eligible trial administered cagrilintide with semaglutide 2.4 mg.",
    "efficacyScore": 2,
    "evidenceQualityScore": 2,
    "safetySummary": "In the eligible phase 1b combination trial, 566 adverse events were reported in 92 participants, including 207 gastrointestinal events (37%); most adverse events were mild to moderate, and the proportion of participants with at least one adverse event was similar across treatment groups. [21] The source interpreted the combination as well tolerated but stated that larger and longer trials are needed to fully assess efficacy and safety. [21]",
    "knownInteractions": "In the eligible trial, cagrilintide was administered with semaglutide 2.4 mg; cagrilintide exposure was proportional to dose and did not affect semaglutide exposure or elimination. [21]",
    "regulatoryStatus": "Not verified in reviewed sources. [19, 20, 21]",
    "limitations": "Only the supplied phase 1b record was counted as an eligible primary human outcome study. The development paper did not provide a verifiable primary human outcome dataset in the supplied record. The cagrilintide-plus-semaglutide article was not counted as an additional primary human study because its supplied summary reports findings from other phase 2 trials rather than presenting a distinct primary trial dataset. The eligible trial was short, single-centre, involved otherwise healthy adults, and evaluated cagrilintide only with semaglutide; larger and longer studies are needed. [21] [21] [21] [21] [21] [21] [21] [21] [21]",
    "missingFields": "For the eligible trial, the supplied source did not report a detailed per-dose adverse-event breakdown, participant-level disposition by every cagrilintide dose, or a separately verified cagrilintide-monotherapy outcome. These fields were not inferred. [21] [21] [21] [21] [21] [21] [21] [21] [21]",
