    "evidenceByGoal": "Acute clinical hepatitis — randomized, placebo-controlled, blinded trial at two hospitals in Egypt; 105 eligible patients with symptoms compatible with acute clinical hepatitis and ALT levels greater than 2.5 times the upper limit of normal. Participants received silymarin 140 mg three times daily for four weeks, with an additional four-week follow-up, versus a vitamin placebo. Silymarin was associated with quicker resolution of dark urine, jaundice, and scleral icterus, and reduced indirect bilirubin; direct bilirubin, ALT, and AST were not significantly reduced. No adverse events were noted and both groups were well tolerated. Key limitations were the modest sample size and multiple etiologies of acute clinical hepatitis; the study did not detect an effect on biomarkers of the underlying hepatocellular inflammatory process. [40]",
    "efficacyScore": 2,
    "evidenceQualityScore": 2,
    "safetySummary": "In the one eligible randomized trial, no adverse events were noted, and both silymarin and placebo were well tolerated. This finding is limited to that trial and does not establish broader safety. [40]",
