    "evidenceByGoal": "Eligible primary human outcome studies reviewed: 3. The three records concern meldonium/Mildronate and were treated as the same active ingredient; evidence was not transferred across other formulations or analogs.\n\n1. Chronic cerebrovascular disease/neuroprotection: open comparative human study in patients with chronic cerebral vascular disease caused by arterial hypertension and atherosclerosis; 30 patients received meldonium 1000 mg/day plus routine basic therapy and 20 controls received routine basic therapy only, for 60 days. [52] Outcomes included clinical symptoms, neurological, psychoemotional and cognitive status, quality of life, cognitive P300 evoked potentials, anxiety, and endothelial markers; the source reported improvements in clinical symptoms and cognitive functions, reduced state and trait anxiety, improved quality of life, and decreases in endothelin-1 and PAI-1. [52] Adverse events: not verified in reviewed source. Key limitation: the source describes an open comparative study; blinding and allocation details were not verified in reviewed source. [52]\n\n2. Early post-myocardial-infarction period/post-MI cardiosclerosis: randomized study of 67 patients aged 40–70 years after myocardial infarction who were discharged for ambulatory supervision; 32 received basic ischemic-heart-disease therapy and 35 received basic therapy plus mildronate for 12 weeks. [53] Dose: not verified in reviewed source. Reported outcomes were fewer angina attacks, fewer epiventricular extrasystoles and paroxysmal rhythm disturbances, lower systolic and diastolic arterial blood pressure, improved quality of life, and lower anxiety. [53] Adverse events: no side effects were registered during mildronate treatment. [53] Key limitation: the dose and further design/reporting details were not verified in reviewed source.\n\n3. Stable angina/anti-ischemic and antioxidant activity: human study in 82 patients with stable angina of effort, functional class II (43 patients) or III (39 patients), mean age 53.3±7.5 years; 71 were men, 11 women, and 21 had concomitant hypertension. [54] All patients received traditional anti-anginal, antiplatelet/disaggregant, and hypolipidemic therapy; one group received Mildronate 750 mg/day for 2 months, followed by a 3-month washout and rotation so the previously untreated group also received 750 mg/day for 2 months. [54] The source reports cycle-ergometer physical-tolerance testing and blood diene-conjugate and malondialdehyde measurements; meldonium was associated with increased physical tolerance, reduced diene-conjugate and malondialdehyde levels, fewer angina attacks, and lower sublingual-nitrate demand. [54] Adverse events: not verified in reviewed source. Key limitation: the source summary does not verify blinding, randomization, or the statistical analysis.\n\nOverall conclusion: limited positive findings were reported for the three distinct clinical settings above, but a stronger cross-condition clinical conclusion was not verified in the reviewed sources.",
    "efficacyScore": 3,
    "evidenceQualityScore": 3,
    "safetySummary": "One reviewed study reported that no side effects were registered during mildronate treatment; broader safety findings across the reviewed records were not verified in reviewed source. [53]",
    "knownInteractions": "Not verified in reviewed sources. [52, 53, 54]",
    "regulatoryStatus": "Not verified in reviewed sources. [52, 53, 54]",
    "limitations": "Evidence is limited to three small candidate records: an open comparative cerebrovascular study, a randomized post-myocardial-infarction study with dose not reported in the reviewed summary, and a stable-angina rotation study with limited reporting of randomization, blinding, and statistical analysis. No evidence was transferred beyond these reviewed records. [52] [52] [52] [53] [53] [53] [54] [54] [54]",
    "missingFields": "For the post-myocardial-infarction study, dose was not verified in the reviewed source. For the cerebrovascular and stable-angina studies, adverse-event details were not verified in the reviewed source. Blinding, allocation, and statistical-analysis details were not fully verified for the reviewed records. [52] [52] [52] [53] [53] [53] [54] [54] [54]",
