    "evidenceByGoal": "Healthy-subject safety, pharmacokinetics, and retinal function: an open-label human trial enrolled 16 healthy subjects and administered 3.0 g/day of L-ornithine hydrochloride for 3 months. [64] No comparator beyond baseline is reported. Plasma ornithine levels, pharmacokinetic parameters after a 1.0 g oral loading dose, clinical and biological indices, and flash electroretinography were assessed; after supplementation, plasma ornithine exposure changed transiently, while other pharmacokinetic parameters and electroretinography results did not change. [64] No clinically relevant side effects were reported. [64] A key limitation is that the study was open-label and conducted in healthy subjects; this limitation is based on the reported design and population. [64]\n\nCircadian phase: a randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled crossover trial included 28 healthy adults aged 20 years or older. [65] Participants took 400 mg of L-ornithine or placebo for 7 days in each period, separated by a 7-day interval. [65] Dim-light melatonin onset was delayed by 15 minutes after L-ornithine ingestion. [65] Adverse events and a key study limitation were not verified in the reviewed source.\n\nMental stress and fatigue after social stress: a randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled, parallel-group trial assigned 65 participants to oral L-ornithine 1600 mg or placebo for 7 days; participants took the product 1 hour before the Trier Social Stress Test on the test day. [66] L-ornithine did not affect salivary cortisol but significantly improved the Profile of Mood States fatigue–inertia and anger–hostility scales the morning after the stress test versus placebo. [66] Adverse events and a key study limitation were not verified in the reviewed source.",
