    "humanStudyCount": 2,
    "totalStudyCount": 3,
    "evidenceByGoal": "Hair growth in androgenetic alopecia: Eligible study: randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial in 76 men with mild to moderate androgenetic alopecia. [88] Dose and duration: pumpkin seed oil 400 mg/day for 24 weeks, with placebo as comparator. [88] Outcomes: standardized clinical-photograph assessment, patient self-assessment, scalp hair thickness, and scalp hair counts were evaluated; at 24 weeks, self-rated improvement and satisfaction were higher with pumpkin seed oil, and mean hair-count increases were 40% versus 10% with placebo. [88] Adverse events: adverse effects were not different between groups. [88] Key limitation: not verified in reviewed source. Benign prostatic hyperplasia: Eligible study: randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial over 12 months in 47 men with BPH, mean age 53.3 years, and baseline IPSS above 8. [89] Dose and comparators: pumpkin seed oil 320 mg/day; comparator arms included placebo, saw palmetto oil 320 mg/day, and pumpkin seed oil plus saw palmetto oil, with each oil at 320 mg/day in the combination arm. [89] Outcomes: IPSS decreased by 3 months in the pumpkin-seed-oil group; quality of life improved by 3 months; prostate volume did not differ; and maximal urinary flow rate improved with statistical significance after 6 months. [89] The source also reports that PSA reduction occurred only in the combination group and that no parameters were significantly improved by the combination treatment. [89] Adverse events: specific adverse events were not reported in the reviewed source; the source states that pumpkin seed oil and saw palmetto oil administrations were clinically safe. [89] Key limitation: not verified in reviewed source. Formulation boundary: the third record studied a proprietary oil-free hydroethanolic pumpkin seed extract in a single-arm pilot study, not pumpkin seed oil; its findings were therefore not transferred to this catalogue ingredient. [90]",
    "efficacyScore": 3,
    "evidenceQualityScore": 3,
    "safetySummary": "In the hair-growth trial, adverse effects were not different between pumpkin seed oil and placebo groups. [88] The BPH source states that administrations of pumpkin seed oil and saw palmetto oil were clinically safe, but does not provide specific adverse-event data in the reviewed summary. [89] The third record concerns an oil-free hydroethanolic pumpkin seed extract rather than pumpkin seed oil, so its tolerability result was not applied to pumpkin seed oil. [90]",
    "knownInteractions": "Not verified in reviewed sources. [88, 89, 90]",
    "regulatoryStatus": "Not verified in reviewed sources. [88, 89, 90]",
    "limitations": "Only the three supplied records were reviewed. Two records were counted as eligible primary human studies for pumpkin seed oil. The oil-free hydroethanolic pumpkin seed extract record was not counted or used for efficacy/safety conclusions about pumpkin seed oil because it is a different formulation. Study-specific key limitations, detailed adverse-event data, interaction data, and regulatory information were not verified in the supplied summaries. [88] [88] [88] [88] [89] [89] [89] [89] [89] [90]",
