    "evidenceByGoal": "Alertness/wakefulness and sustained attention during acute nocturnal sleep loss: one double-blind, active- and placebo-controlled, parallel-group primary human study enrolled 107 healthy male volunteers aged 18–40 years and administered a single oral dose of armodafinil 100, 150, 200, or 300 mg, with modafinil 200 mg or placebo as comparators. [91] The primary outcome was the Maintenance of Wakefulness Test administered every 2 hours from 22:00 to 08:00; secondary outcomes were the Psychomotor Vigilance Task and Karolinska Sleepiness Scale. [91] Armodafinil improved wakefulness at all studied doses and reduced PVT lapses of attention; later effects appeared dose- and concentration-dependent. [91] Adverse events were assessed during the 2-day laboratory stay and by telephone on day 9. [91] Reported adverse events included abdominal pain, nausea, headache, tachycardia/palpitations, decreased mean sleep efficiency, and increased mean blood pressure. [91] Key limitation: the source states that further research on efficacy, safety, and tolerability in patients with disorders of excessive sleepiness is required; this study was conducted in healthy volunteers during acute sleep loss, not in a patient population. [91]",
