    "evidenceByGoal": "Memory complaint/cognitive and attention outcomes in adults over 50: randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled, parallel-group therapeutic trial in patients with memory complaints seen in general practice. Of 626 initially included subjects, 462 were retained for final analysis; the analyzed groups were adrafinil (n=225) and placebo (n=237). Adrafinil was given at 900 mg per 24 hours for three months, versus placebo. The adrafinil group had a significantly greater improvement in memory complaint than placebo (p<0.0001), with the Mac Nair score changing from 40.5 to 26.8 versus 38.1 to 31; subjective visual-analog ratings of memory, attention, and orientation, as well as investigator-rated efficacy and CGI, also improved significantly. Crocq-battery memory and attention measures likewise improved significantly. Adverse events were not reported in the reviewed source. A stated limitation was the absence of quantitative anxiety assessment, so the mechanism could not be determined with certainty. [99]",
