    "evidenceByGoal": "Safety/tolerability, pharmacokinetics, androgen-receptor engagement, and antitumor activity in ER+/HER2- metastatic breast cancer: Design: first-in-human phase 1 dose-escalation study with a 3+3 design and a PK expansion cohort [114]. Population: postmenopausal women with ER+/HER2- metastatic breast cancer; 22 heavily pretreated patients were enrolled, including 21 AR+ patients [114]. Dose: 50 mg (n=6), 100 mg (n=13), or 150 mg (n=3) once daily [114]. Duration: not verified in reviewed source. Comparator: not verified in reviewed source. Outcomes: at the 100 mg/day maximum tolerated dose, 1 patient with a baseline ESR1 mutation had a partial response [114]; clinical benefit rate at 24 weeks was 18.2% and median progression-free survival was 2.3 months [114]; SHBG decreased in 18/18 patients, PSA increased in 16/20 patients, and paired biopsies demonstrated androgen-receptor engagement [114]. Key limitation: the reviewed outcome report is a small dose-escalation phase 1 study, and duration and comparator were not verified in the reviewed source. Adverse events are summarized separately in the safety field.",
    "efficacyScore": 2,
    "evidenceQualityScore": 2,
    "safetySummary": "In the eligible phase 1 study, treatment-emergent adverse events occurring in more than 10% of patients included elevated AST (59.1%), elevated ALT (45.5%), elevated total blood bilirubin (27.3%), vomiting, dehydration, decreased appetite, and weight loss (27.3% each) [114]. Grade 3/4 treatment-emergent adverse events occurred in 16/22 patients (72.7%) and included AST/ALT elevations and hypophosphatemia (22.7% each) [114]. Treatment-related adverse events occurred in 17/22 patients (77.3%); 7/22 (31.8%) were grade 3 and none were grade 4 [114]. These are study-reported adverse events; no broader safety conclusion is verified in the reviewed sources.",
