    "evidenceByGoal": "Goal: lean body mass and physical performance. Eligible study 1 (cancer cachexia): randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial in 159 subjects with non-small-cell lung cancer, colorectal cancer, non-Hodgkin lymphoma, breast cancer, or chronic lymphocytic leukemia; 100 subjects were included in the modified intention-to-treat analysis [121]. Participants received 1 mg Ostarine, 3 mg Ostarine, or matching placebo daily for 16 weeks [121]. The primary outcome was total lean body mass measured by DEXA; secondary outcomes were stair-climb time and power, gait speed, and grip strength [121]. Lean body mass changed from baseline by 0.1 kg with placebo, 1.5 kg with 1 mg, and 1.3 kg with 3 mg [121]; stair-climb power changed by 0.23 watts, 35.2 watts, and 44.7 watts, respectively [121]. Gait speed and grip strength were not different between Ostarine and placebo [121]. Adverse events: not verified in reviewed source. Key limitation: only 100 of the 159 subjects were included in the reported modified intention-to-treat analysis [121]. Eligible study 2: the supplied record identifies Ostarine increases in lean body mass and improved physical performance in healthy elderly subjects; design, sample size, dose, duration, comparator, adverse events, and key limitation: not verified in reviewed source. The MK-0773 sarcopenia trial was excluded because its named compound was MK-0773 rather than MK-2866/Ostarine.",
