    "evidenceByGoal": "Glycemic control: (1) Metformin-treated type 2 diabetes inadequately controlled with diet and metformin: multicenter randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled parallel-group trial. [138] Sample size: not verified in reviewed source. Dose: forced titration from 25–50 mg three times daily, then titration of 50–100 mg three times daily according to glucose control. [138] Duration: 31 weeks overall, including a 24-week acarbose-or-placebo treatment period. [138] Comparator: placebo. [138] Outcome: mean HbA1c decreased by 0.65%; fasting and postprandial plasma glucose and serum insulin also decreased significantly versus placebo. [138] Adverse events: gastrointestinal side effects were more frequent with acarbose; liver transaminase elevations did not differ significantly from placebo. [138] Key limitation: not verified in reviewed source. (2) Asian type 2 diabetic patients on stable insulin doses: multinational, double-blind, placebo-controlled parallel-arm study. [139] Sample size: not verified in reviewed source. Dose: 50 mg three times daily for 6 weeks, followed by 100 mg three times daily for 12 weeks. [139] Duration: 18 weeks of treatment, preceded by a 2-week screening phase. [139] Comparator: matching placebo. [139] Outcome: HbA1c favored acarbose by −0.69% versus placebo (95% CI −1.18 to −0.2; P=0.008); 1-hour postprandial glucose also decreased (P=0.029), while fasting glucose, triglycerides, and cholesterol did not differ. [139] Adverse events: safety profiles were similar except for more flatulence with acarbose (28.6% versus 16.4% with placebo). [139] Key limitation: not verified in reviewed source. (3) Non-insulin-dependent diabetic patients treated with diet or diet plus sulfonylureas: multicenter, double-blind, randomized, placebo-controlled crossover trial in 90 patients. [140] Dose: 50 mg/day during the first month of each phase, followed by 100 mg/day for 2 months. [140] Duration: 3 months of initial treatment, 1-month placebo washout, then 3 months of the inverse treatment. [140] Comparator: placebo and crossover inverse treatment. [140] Outcome: HbA1 and postprandial glycemia improved significantly; triglycerides, cholesterol, fasting glycemia, and postprandial insulinemia showed nonsignificant differences. [140] Adverse events: hypoglycemia occurred in 12 patients and undesirable gastrointestinal effects were reported by 57 patients. [140] Key limitation: not verified in reviewed source.",
    "efficacyScore": 4,
    "evidenceQualityScore": 3,
    "safetySummary": "In the metformin-treated trial, gastrointestinal side effects were more frequent with acarbose, with no significant difference from placebo in liver transaminase elevations. [138] In the insulin-treated Asian trial, safety profiles were similar except for flatulence, reported in 28.6% with acarbose versus 16.4% with placebo. [139] In the crossover trial, hypoglycemia occurred in 12 patients and undesirable gastrointestinal effects were reported by 57 patients. [140]",
