    "humanStudyCount": 3,
    "totalStudyCount": 3,
    "evidenceByGoal": "Three distinct primary human randomized studies were reviewed; findings were kept formulation-specific.\n\n1. Prostate-cancer biomarkers before prostatectomy — Phase II randomized controlled trial in 45 men with clinically localized prostate cancer; participants received 15, 30, or 45 mg lycopene, or no supplement, from biopsy to prostatectomy [150]. Forty-two of 45 completed approximately 30 days of intervention [150]. Plasma lycopene increased in all treatment groups, with the greatest increase in the 45-mg group; serum free testosterone decreased with 30 mg and total estradiol increased with 30 and 45 mg, while PSA and tissue Ki-67 did not significantly increase [150]. The changes were not significant versus the control arm given the sample size and intervention duration [150]. Comparator: no supplement. Adverse events: the source reported no toxicity [150]. Key limitation: sample size and duration, as explicitly noted by the source [150].\n\n2. Prostate/plasma lycopene and oxidative-stress markers — Double-blind randomized placebo-controlled trial in 105 African American male veterans recommended for prostate biopsy; 47 had prostate cancer and 58 had benign prostatic hyperplasia [151]. Dose: 30 mg/day lycopene as tomato oleoresin; duration: 21 days before biopsy; comparator: placebo [151]. Plasma and prostate-tissue lycopene concentrations increased, but no significant changes were observed in prostate-tissue 8-oxo-deoxyguanosine or plasma malondialdehyde [151]. Adverse events: not verified in reviewed source. Key limitation: not verified in reviewed source.\n\n3. Prostate-cancer pathological and biomarker outcomes — Randomized clinical trial in 26 men with prostate cancer, with 15 assigned to intervention and the remainder to no supplement, for three weeks before radical prostatectomy [152]. The intervention was a tomato oleoresin capsule containing 15 mg lycopene plus phytoene, phytofluene, and tocopherol, taken twice daily with meals; this was not a lycopene-only formulation [152]. Reported outcomes included tumor size, organ-confined disease, surgical-margin/extrap prostatic involvement, HGPIN, tissue proliferation/apoptosis biomarkers, oxidative stress, PSA, IGF-1, and IGFBP-3; the intervention group had more men with smaller tumors, organ-confined disease, and focal HGPIN, and lower mean plasma PSA than controls [152]. Adverse events: not verified in reviewed source. Key limitation: the source characterizes it as a pilot study and states that larger clinical trials are necessary [152].",
    "efficacyScore": 2,
    "evidenceQualityScore": 2,
    "safetySummary": "The 15/30/45-mg localized-prostate-cancer trial reported no toxicity [150]. Adverse-event information was not verified in the other two reviewed sources.",
