    "evidenceByGoal": "Knee osteoarthritis symptoms and pain — Record 1: randomized, placebo-controlled 8-week trial of glucosamine hydrochloride in patients with knee osteoarthritis; sample size, dose, and adverse events were not verified in the reviewed source. Acetaminophen was prescribed for pain, with placebo as comparator. The primary WOMAC pain endpoint was negative, although some secondary diary and examination measures favored glucosamine; the key limitation was failure to meet the primary endpoint. [171]\n\nKnee osteoarthritis clinical symptoms and biochemical markers — Record 3: randomized, single-blinded controlled clinical trial in 44 patients aged 40–70 years, allocated 22 per group, over 6 months. The intervention was oral glucosamine hydrochloride plus chondroitin sulfate; dose and adverse events were not verified in the reviewed source. The comparator was home exercise plus oral paracetamol. VAS and WOMAC scores improved significantly in the intervention group, while collagen-II and CTX-II marker results did not differ significantly between groups. The key limitations were that the combination does not isolate glucosamine's effect and that the trial was single-blinded. [173]\n\nRecord 2 was not counted as an eligible primary human outcome study because the supplied summary consists of a citation to another trial and background text rather than a verifiable report of its own primary human outcomes; its glucosamine sulfate evidence was not transferred to the hydrochloride or combination formulations.",
