    "evidenceByGoal": "Fatigue after blood donation in non-anemic, iron-deficient female donors: randomized controlled trial; 154 women aged below 50 years; oral ferrous sulfate versus placebo for four weeks. [180] Fatigue was the main outcome, and no significant clinical effect was observed; the summary reports a fatigue change of -0.15 points (95% CI -0.9 to 0.6; P=0.697). [180] Hemoglobin and ferritin increased, while compliance and interruption for side effects were similar between groups. [180] Dose and key limitation were not verified in the reviewed source. Heart-transplant recipients with iron deficiency: randomized, placebo-controlled, double-blind trial; 102 recipients at least one year after transplantation; ferric derisomaltose 20 mg/kg versus placebo, assessed at six months. [180] The primary outcome was peak oxygen consumption, which did not improve overall; in the subgroup with baseline ferritin below 30 micrograms/L, peak oxygen consumption was significantly higher with ferric derisomaltose. Iron stores were restored in 86% versus 20%, and quality of life was significantly better with ferric derisomaltose. [180] Twenty-seven adverse events occurred with intravenous iron versus 30 with placebo (P=0.39). [180] Key limitation was not verified in the reviewed source. The third candidate was a trial protocol rather than a primary human outcome report and was not counted. [181] Evidence was not transferred between the oral ferrous sulfate and intravenous ferric derisomaltose formulations.",
