    "category": "Amino acid supplement [183, 184, 185]",
    "latestVerifiedPublicationYear": 1984,
    "humanStudyCount": 3,
    "totalStudyCount": 3,
    "evidenceByGoal": "Recurrent herpes simplex labialis recurrence and related lesion outcomes — 3 eligible, uniquely identifiable primary human studies were reviewed. Thein and Hurt: 26 volunteers with frequently recurring lesions completed a 12-month double-blind crossover study; the intervention was 1,000 mg oral 1-lysine daily, compared with the crossover control condition. The summary reports fewer lesions in most lysine-group participants and increased lesion frequency after withdrawal; recurrence was lower when serum lysine exceeded 165 nmol/ml. Adverse events were not reported in the reviewed summary. Key limitation: the conclusion was framed as potentially useful only in selected cases if serum lysine concentrations could be maintained. [183] Milman, Scheibel, and Jessen: 65 patients participated in a double-blind, placebo-controlled crossover study of 1,000 mg lysine monohydrochloride daily; lysine was given for 12 weeks followed by placebo for 12 weeks. Overall recurrence rate was not reduced, although significantly more patients were recurrence-free during lysine than placebo treatment (p = 0.05). No effect was reported on healing rate or lesion appearance at worst. Adverse events were not reported in the reviewed summary. Key limitation: the overall recurrence result was negative and any benefit appeared limited to certain patients. [183] McCune and colleagues: 41 nonimmunocompromised patients participated in a prospective randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled crossover study. The summary reports 1,248 mg/day of L-lysine monohydrochloride as associated with decreased recurrence, while 624 mg/day was not effective; it also reports possible reduction in symptom severity, but neither dose shortened healing time versus placebo. Treatment duration was not reported in the reviewed summary. Adverse events were not reported in the reviewed summary. Key limitation: the summary does not provide the treatment duration or detailed outcome data, and the lower dose was ineffective. [183]",
