    "evidenceByGoal": "Goal: body weight and body-fat reduction. Three eligible primary human controlled studies were identified, and their formulations should not be treated as interchangeable. (1) Dietary MCT versus LCT: double-blind controlled study in 78 healthy men and women, stratified by BMI; the record reports a 12-week treatment, consumption of 9,218 kJ/day and 60 g/day total fat, and LCT as comparator, but does not separately verify the MCT dose. Body weight and body fat decreased in both groups; among participants with BMI ≥23 kg/m², MCT produced a greater body-weight decrease, greater body-fat loss at 8 weeks (-3.86 ± 0.3 kg vs -2.75 ± 0.2 kg), and greater subcutaneous-fat reduction at weeks 4, 8, and 12. [186] Adverse events: not verified in reviewed source. Key limitation: not verified in reviewed source. (2) MCT test oil versus corn oil/LCT: pilot randomized study in 40 free-living, moderately overweight Chinese adults with type 2 diabetes; 18 g/day for 90 days, with corn oil as control. The MCT group had reductions in body weight and waist circumference, and these outcomes were lower than in the LCT group at study end. [187] Adverse events: not verified in reviewed source. Key limitation: not verified in reviewed source. (3) MCT-containing margarine versus LCT margarine: double-blind controlled study in 73 subjects for 12 weeks; 14 g/day test margarine containing 5 g/day MCT versus 5 g/day LCT, with 2,100–2,400 kcal/day and 65–73 g/day total fat. MCT was associated with greater reductions in body-fat weight (-3.8 ± 2.4 kg vs -2.4 ± 1.7 kg), subcutaneous fat (-38.2 ± 29.9 cm² vs -22.6 ± 19.3 cm²), and visceral fat (-12.2 ± 11.2 cm² vs -1.6 ± 12.8 cm²) after 12 weeks. [188] Adverse events: not verified in reviewed source. Key limitation: not verified in reviewed source.\n\nGoal: insulin sensitivity and related metabolic risk factors. One eligible primary human study was identified: in the 40-person type 2 diabetes pilot study, MCT was given at 18 g/day for 90 days versus corn oil/LCT. The record reports increased serum C-peptide, reduced HOMA-IR, and decreased serum cholesterol in the MCT group; it also reports an involuntary reduction in energy intake in that group. [187] Adverse events: not verified in reviewed source. Key limitation: not verified in reviewed source.\n\nThe supplied records do not verify clinical adverse events or study-specific key limitations for these outcomes.",
    "efficacyScore": 3,
    "evidenceQualityScore": 3,
    "safetySummary": "Not verified in reviewed sources. [186, 187, 188]",
    "knownInteractions": "Not verified in reviewed sources. [186, 187, 188]",
    "regulatoryStatus": "Not verified in reviewed sources. [186, 187, 188]",
    "limitations": "The audit reviewed exactly 3 supplied candidate records. The records report three primary human controlled studies, but adverse events and study-specific key limitations are not reported in the supplied summaries. The first record does not separately state the MCT dose; the second does not report group sizes; and formulation-specific results were not transferred across dietary MCT, MCT test oil, and MCT-containing margarine. [186] [187] [188] [187]",
