    "evidenceByGoal": "Goal: muscle performance, muscle strength, muscle mass, and muscle thickness during resistance/weight training. Study 1 (record 18d58c47f6ef2f86a1418605875d7eb5): design: randomized, comparator-controlled, parallel clinical trial; population: sedentary adults; intervention/comparator: pea protein versus whey protein, both combined with a weekly resistance-training program; duration: 84 days. [189] Sample size: not verified in reviewed source. Dose: not verified in reviewed source. Outcomes: whole-body muscle strength, including handgrip, lower-body, and upper-body strength, body composition, and product perception were assessed. [189] Pea-protein participants had a 16.1% improvement in whole-body muscle strength after 84 days, while the whey-protein group had an 11.1% improvement. [189] There were no significant between-group differences in changes in whole-body muscle strength, muscle mass, or product perception and likability. [189] Adverse events/safety: both study products were reported as safe and well-tolerated in the enrolled population. [189] Key limitation: not verified in reviewed source. Study 2 (record 18d58c47f6ecced6d26155dba12077bd): design: double-blind, randomized, placebo-controlled clinical trial; population: not verified in reviewed source; sample size: not verified in reviewed source; intervention/dose: oral pea protein, dose not verified in reviewed source; comparator: whey protein and/or placebo as described in the record, with the comparator structure not further verifiable from the supplied summary; duration: not verified in reviewed source. The record reports that oral pea protein increased muscle thickness during weight training more than whey protein. [190] Outcome measure: muscle thickness. [190] Adverse events: not verified in reviewed source. Key limitation: not verified in reviewed source. Study 3 (record 18d58c47f6f0bde3f4c0b6fc0f618549) is a study-design-and-rationale paper for a planned trial rather than a verified primary human outcome report. [191]",
