    "humanStudyCount": 2,
    "totalStudyCount": 3,
    "evidenceByGoal": "Glycemic control: (1) Randomized, placebo-controlled study in 34 men with type 2 diabetes and mild-to-moderate hypercholesterolemia after a 2-week dietary stabilization phase; psyllium 5.1 g versus cellulose placebo twice daily for 8 weeks. The reported outcomes were serum lipid and glycemic indexes, including all-day and postlunch postprandial glucose; compared with placebo, these were 11.0% and 19.2% lower, respectively. Both products were well tolerated, with no serious treatment-related adverse events reported. [192] (2) Double-blind, randomized, placebo-controlled study in 33 patients with type II diabetes and baseline HbA1c 7–9% and fasting blood glucose 135–239 mg/dL; placebo (n=8), psyllium 3.4 g (n=13), or 6.8 g (n=12) twice daily before a meal for 12 weeks. Fasting blood glucose was assessed at weeks 2, 4, 8, and 12, and HbA1c at weeks 4, 8, and 12. Psyllium significantly lowered fasting blood glucose versus placebo at weeks 4–12 and HbA1c versus placebo at week 12; the reported HbA1c mean differences were −0.53 for 3.4 g twice daily and −0.66 for 6.8 g twice daily. Adverse events and a study-specific key limitation were not reported in the reviewed source. [194, 193]\n\nLipid control: In the 34-man randomized study described above, serum total cholesterol was 8.9% lower with psyllium than placebo, while LDL cholesterol was 13.0% lower with psyllium, with P=0.07 for the LDL comparison. The study population, dose, duration, comparator, adverse-event finding, and limitation are as stated above; no additional eligible primary human lipid study was counted because the other cholesterol record was a meta-analysis. [192]",
    "efficacyScore": 3,
    "evidenceQualityScore": 3,
    "safetySummary": "In the 1999 randomized study, both psyllium and cellulose placebo were reported as well tolerated, with no serious treatment-related adverse events in either group. [192] Adverse-event findings were not reported in the supplied summary for the 2011 randomized study, and no safety conclusion beyond these reported findings is made. [194]",
    "knownInteractions": "Not verified in reviewed sources. [192, 193, 194]",
    "regulatoryStatus": "Not verified in reviewed sources. [192, 193, 194]",
    "limitations": "Only three supplied records were reviewed. One was a meta-analysis and was not counted as a primary human study; two randomized primary human records were counted. The eligible studies were small, and adverse-event details and study-specific key limitations were not reported for the 2011 record. No evidence was transferred from the meta-analysis into the primary-study count. [192] [194, 193] [192]",
    "missingFields": "For the 2011 randomized study, adverse events and a study-specific key limitation were not reported in the supplied source summary. Interactions and regulatory status were not reported in the reviewed sources. [192] [194, 193] [192]",
