    "humanStudyCount": 2,
    "totalStudyCount": 3,
    "evidenceByGoal": "Major depressive disorder: one double-blind, randomized, placebo-controlled trial studied 189 outpatients with DSM-IV major depressive disorder, randomized for 12 weeks to SAMe 1,600–3,200 mg/day, escitalopram 10–20 mg/day, or placebo. [198] The outcome was HDRS-17; all three groups improved by about 5–6 points, but there were no significant differences between groups, and the reported response rates were 36% for SAMe, 34% for escitalopram, and 30% for placebo. [198] Adverse events in the SAMe arm most commonly included stomach discomfort (19%) and diarrhea (20%); differences between groups were also reported for dizziness, anorgasmia, diminished mental acuity, and hot flashes. [198] Key limitation: the trial's conclusion was that the results did not support an advantage over placebo. [198]\n\nOsteoarthritis: one 24-month multicenter open trial studied 108 patients with knee, hip, cervical-spine, and dorsal/lumbar-spine osteoarthritis. [199] Participants received 600 mg/day for the first two weeks and then 400 mg/day through month 24; symptoms assessed included morning stiffness, pain at rest, and pain on movement. [199] The source reported improvement beginning in the first weeks and continuing through month 24. [199] Adverse events were reported as nonspecific side effects in 20 patients, with no treatment discontinuations; no pathologic laboratory changes were reported. [199] Comparator: not verified in reviewed source. Key limitation: this was an open trial rather than a blinded controlled comparison. [199]",
    "efficacyScore": 2,
    "evidenceQualityScore": 2,
    "safetySummary": "In the major-depressive-disorder trial, tolerability was described as good; the most common SAMe-arm gastrointestinal adverse events were stomach discomfort (19%) and diarrhea (20%), and significant between-group differences were reported for dizziness, anorgasmia, diminished mental acuity, and hot flashes. [198, 199] In the 24-month osteoarthritis open trial, nonspecific side effects occurred in 20 patients, no patient discontinued therapy because of them, and no pathologic laboratory changes were reported at scheduled testing. These findings are limited to the reviewed studies and do not establish broader safety or interaction conclusions.",
    "knownInteractions": "Not mentioned in source. [198, 199, 200]",
