    "evidenceByGoal": "Metabolic acidosis in critically ill adults: investigator-initiated, parallel-group, pilot randomized double-blind trial across eight ICUs; 30 adults admitted within 48 hours, receiving a vasopressor and meeting specified metabolic-acidosis criteria. The intervention was sodium bicarbonate versus 5% dextrose placebo; dose and treatment duration were not verified in the reviewed source. The primary clinical outcome was hours alive and free of vasopressors on day 7, with median values of 132.2 versus 97.1 hours and a nonsignificant median difference of 35.07 hours (p = 0.131). Biochemical outcomes favored sodium bicarbonate for time to base-excess and pH correction, and recurrence of metabolic acidosis was lower. No adverse events were reported. Key limitation: pilot feasibility design, small sample, nonsignificant primary clinical outcome, and recruitment feasibility concerns; the source states that eligibility criteria may require modification. [201]\n\nLong-term consumption/tolerability in healthy volunteers: Phase 0/I clinical trial with 15 volunteers (11 women and 4 men; average age 55 years). Participants were instructed to consume up to 0.5 g/kg/day of sodium bicarbonate for 90 days, with permission to reduce the dose; the average daily dose was 0.17 ± 0.03 g/kg. No comparator was reported. Outcomes included adherence feasibility, vital signs, basic metabolic panels, urine pH, and adverse-event monitoring. Most adverse events were Grade 1 and gastrointestinal; two were Grade 2, two participants withdrew before day 90, and one case of metabolic alkalosis resolved after dose reduction. Key limitation: small, apparently uncontrolled study in healthy volunteers, with self-adjusted dosing and no disease-treatment efficacy endpoint. [202]\n\nThe third candidate record provides only a ClinicalTrials.gov registration statement and no verifiable study design, population, intervention, outcomes, or results in the reviewed source; it therefore contributes zero eligible human studies.",
    "efficacyScore": 2,
    "evidenceQualityScore": 2,
    "safetySummary": "In the ICU pilot, no adverse events were reported. [201] In the healthy-volunteer study, most adverse events were Grade 1 and gastrointestinal, two were Grade 2, two participants withdrew before day 90, and one case of metabolic alkalosis resolved after downward dose adjustment. [202] These findings are limited to the two reviewed studies and do not establish general safety.",
    "knownInteractions": "Not verified in reviewed sources. [201, 202, 203]",
    "regulatoryStatus": "Not verified in reviewed sources. [201, 202, 203]",
    "limitations": "Only the three supplied records were reviewed. The evidence is limited to the reported populations and formulations of these records; no evidence was transferred from other sodium-bicarbonate formulations or analogs. The third candidate contained only a registration statement and was not counted as an eligible study. Dose was not reported in the reviewed summary for the ICU trial, and comparator, duration, and several other requested fields were not reported for the healthy-volunteer study. [201] [202]",
    "missingFields": "ICU pilot: dose and duration were not verified in the reviewed source. Healthy-volunteer study: comparator was not reported; disease-treatment efficacy was not assessed in the reviewed summary. Candidate 3: study design, population, dose, duration, comparator, outcomes, adverse events, and limitations were not verifiable from the supplied record. [201] [202]",
