    "evidenceByGoal": "Arthralgia/pain in nonmetastatic hormone-positive breast cancer patients receiving aromatase inhibitors: randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial; 48 patients were included in the final analysis (tart cherry 23, placebo 25), after 60 enrolled. Participants consumed 1 oz tart cherry concentrate diluted in 8 oz water daily for 6 weeks; placebo was syrup. Mean pain decreased 34.7% with tart cherry versus 1.4% with placebo, with a reported P value of .034. The source reports that two patients did not complete because of diarrhea and that 10 were noncompliant. Key limitations reported or evident from the record are the small analyzed sample, attrition/noncompliance, and the narrowly defined breast-cancer population. [204]\n\nCardiovascular/metabolic biomarkers in adults with metabolic syndrome: randomized, parallel-arm, single-blind, placebo-controlled pilot clinical trial; 19 adults aged 20–60 years participated. The tart cherry group consumed 480 mL daily for 12 weeks and was compared with an isocaloric placebo-control drink. At 12 weeks, oxidized LDL and soluble VCAM-1 were significantly lower with tart cherry than control; total cholesterol showed a trend toward being lower, while brachial blood pressure, aortic hemodynamics, and arterial stiffness did not improve. The key limitation stated by the source is that further research is needed; the record is also a small pilot study. Adverse events were not reported in the reviewed source. [205]\n\nUlcerative colitis: no eligible primary human outcome study was identified because the record is explicitly a protocol; its planned trial was to recruit 40 participants, last 6 weeks, and assess quality of life and other outcomes, so these are not results. [205]",
    "efficacyScore": 3,
    "evidenceQualityScore": 2,
    "safetySummary": "Two patients in the breast-cancer trial did not complete because of diarrhea. [204] Broader safety findings were not verified in the reviewed sources.",
