    "humanStudyCount": 2,
    "totalStudyCount": 3,
    "evidenceByGoal": "Body-fat mass in overweight or obese adults:\n\n• 2000 randomized, double-blind study: 60 overweight or obese volunteers (BMI 25–35 kg/m²) received placebo (9 g olive oil) or 1.7, 3.4, 5.1, or 6.8 g CLA/day for 12 weeks; body composition was measured by DXA at baseline, week 6, and week 12. Forty-seven participants completed. [225]\n• The study reported a significantly greater reduction in body-fat mass in the CLA groups than in the placebo group (P=0.03); reductions within groups were significant for 3.4 and 6.8 g/day, with no additional effect above 3.4 g/day. [225]\n• Adverse events: eight participants withdrew because of adverse events, but adverse events did not differ among treatment groups. [225]\n• Key limitation: only 47 of 60 participants completed the 12-week study. [225]\n\n• 2004 double-blind, placebo-controlled 1-year study: 180 healthy overweight adults (BMI 25–30 kg/m²) were randomized to CLA-free fatty acid, CLA-triacylglycerol, or olive-oil placebo. The primary outcome was DXA-measured body-fat mass; secondary outcomes included lean body mass, adverse events, and safety variables. The dose was not verified in the reviewed source. [226]\n• Compared with placebo, body-fat mass was 8.7 ± 9.1% lower with CLA-triacylglycerol and 6.9 ± 9.1% lower with CLA-free fatty acid (P<0.001); CLA-free fatty acid also produced 1.8 ± 4.3% greater lean body mass than placebo (P=0.002). [226]\n• Adverse events did not differ significantly between groups. The source also reported increased LDL with CLA-free fatty acid, decreased HDL with CLA-triacylglycerol, and increased lipoprotein(a) with both CLA groups compared with month 0. [226]\n• Key limitation: the reviewed source reports separate CLA-free-fatty-acid and CLA-triacylglycerol arms, and does not provide the dose. [226]\n\nThe third candidate was a study protocol rather than a completed primary outcome report and therefore contributes zero eligible human outcome studies; it planned 74 adults, 3.0 g of 80% CLA versus 3.0 g linoleic acid daily for 3 months. [227]",
    "efficacyScore": 3,
    "evidenceQualityScore": 3,
    "safetySummary": "In the 12-week study, eight participants withdrew because of adverse events, but adverse events did not differ among treatment groups. [225] In the 1-year study, adverse events did not differ significantly between groups; however, LDL increased with CLA-free fatty acid, HDL decreased with CLA-triacylglycerol, and lipoprotein(a) increased with both CLA groups compared with month 0. [226] A broader safety conclusion is not verified in reviewed sources.",
    "knownInteractions": "Not verified in reviewed sources. [225, 226, 227]",
