    "evidenceByGoal": "Carpal tunnel syndrome: Preliminary clinical trial; randomization and control group were not verified in the reviewed source. Population: 20 patients with carpal tunnel syndrome. Dose: serratiopeptidase 10 mg twice daily, with an initial short course of nimesulide. Duration: 6 weeks. Comparator: not verified in reviewed source. Outcome: 65% showed significant clinical improvement, supported by significant improvement in electrophysiological parameters. Adverse events: no significant side effect was observed; recurrence was reported in four cases. Key limitation: the source states that a larger study is needed. [231]\n\nAnkle sprain: Single-centre prospective comparative trial without randomization. Population: 76 patients aged 18–53 with Grade II ankle sprains, with 38 in each group. Dose: serrapeptase 5 mg, two tablets three times daily. Duration: 10 days. Comparator: paracetamol 500 mg three times daily for 10 days. Outcomes: edema was assessed by Figure-of-Eight and water-displacement methods, and pain by Visual Analogue Scale; serrapeptase produced greater edema reduction on days 3 and 10, while pain management did not differ significantly. Adverse events: not verified in reviewed source. Key limitations: single-centre design and absence of randomization. [231, 232, 233]\n\nThe idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis record was not counted because it studied the branded systemic enzyme supplements Serracor-NK® and Serra Rx260 rather than the exact serrapeptase formulation; its findings were therefore not transferred.",
    "efficacyScore": 3,
    "evidenceQualityScore": 2,
    "safetySummary": "In the carpal-tunnel study, no significant side effect was observed, although recurrence was reported in four cases. [231] Adverse events for the ankle-sprain study and the excluded branded formulations were not verified in the reviewed sources.",
