    "totalStudyCount": 3,
    "evidenceByGoal": "Body fat, visceral fat, and whole-body energy expenditure — Grains of Paradise extract: single-blind, randomized, placebo-controlled crossover study in 19 non-obese female volunteers aged 20–22 years; 30 mg/day orally for 4 weeks; placebo comparator. Visceral fat area decreased slightly with Grains of Paradise and increased slightly with placebo, with a between-condition difference (p<0.05); the change was negatively correlated with initial visceral fat area (r=-0.64, p<0.01). Whole-body energy expenditure increased with Grains of Paradise but not placebo (p<0.05), while subcutaneous and total fat were unaffected. [234] Adverse events: not verified in reviewed source. Key limitation: not verified in reviewed source.\n\nBody composition and hunger — In a double-blind randomized study, 37 healthy overweight or obese men and women completed an exercise and energy-restriction program and received placebo, 500 mg/day L-BAIBA, or 500 mg/day L-BAIBA plus 40 mg/day Grains of Paradise for 8 weeks. Assessments occurred at 0, 2, 4, and 8 weeks using body-mass measures, DXA, and visual analogue scales. The L-BAIBA-plus-Grains-of-Paradise group had reduced hunger ratings versus placebo after 28 days; no significant group-by-time interactions were found for body mass, DXA fat mass, fat-free mass, or percentage fat, and no significant effects were found for appetite, energy, focus, attention, or concentration. [234, 236] Adverse events: not verified in reviewed source. Key limitation: Grains of Paradise was tested only in combination with L-BAIBA, so this record does not isolate the effect of Grains of Paradise. [234]",
