    "totalStudyCount": 3,
    "evidenceByGoal": "Prevention of upper respiratory tract infections: Eligible study: a three-armed, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial in 302 volunteers without acute illness, conducted across four military institutions and one industrial plant. Ethanolic extracts from Echinacea purpurea roots or Echinacea angustifolia roots, or placebo, were given orally for 12 weeks; the source does not report the extract dose. [237] The primary outcome was time to first upper respiratory tract infection, with secondary outcomes including infection occurrence, global assessment, and adverse effects. Time to first infection was 66 days for E. angustifolia, 69 days for E. purpurea, and 65 days for placebo (P=.49); infection occurred in 32.0%, 29.3%, and 36.7%, respectively. [237] Adverse effects were reported by 18 E. angustifolia participants, 10 E. purpurea participants, and 11 placebo participants. [237] The study concluded that a prophylactic effect could not be shown; its key limitation was that a much larger sample would be needed to establish a possible 10%–20% relative risk reduction. [237] Duration, comparator, and outcomes are reported above; dose was not verified in the reviewed source. The 2019 pediatric record was not sufficiently reported in the supplied source to verify its design details, population, sample size, dose, duration, comparator, outcomes, adverse events, or limitation. [238]\n\nTreatment of the common cold: Eligible study: a randomized controlled trial in Dane County, Wisconsin, enrolling 719 patients aged 12–80 years with new-onset common cold; 713 completed the protocol. [239] Participants received no pills, blinded placebo pills, blinded echinacea pills, or unblinded echinacea pills. Echinacea groups received the equivalent of 10.2 g dried echinacea root during the first 24 hours and 5.1 g during each of the next 4 days. [239] The primary outcome was area under the curve for global severity measured with the Wisconsin Upper Respiratory Symptom Survey, short version; secondary outcomes were interleukin-8 and neutrophil counts from nasal wash. [239] Compared with blinded placebo, blinded echinacea showed a nonsignificant 28-point severity trend (P=.089) and a nonsignificant 0.53-day illness-duration difference (P=.075); the source states that illness duration and severity were not statistically significant and that the results did not support a substantive change in the course of the common cold. [239] Adverse events were not mentioned in the reviewed source. The key limitation was higher-than-expected variability, which limited power to detect small benefits. [239]",
    "efficacyScore": 2,
    "evidenceQualityScore": 3,
    "safetySummary": "In the 1998 prevention trial, adverse effects were reported by 18 subjects receiving Echinacea angustifolia, 10 receiving Echinacea purpurea, and 11 receiving placebo. [237] Adverse events were not mentioned in the reviewed source for the 2010 common-cold trial, and no broader safety conclusion is verified from these records.",
