    "totalStudyCount": 3,
    "evidenceByGoal": "Breast-cancer tumor biomarkers: randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial in postmenopausal patients with newly diagnosed breast cancer; 32 participants (19 flaxseed, 13 control); 25 g/day flaxseed muffin versus placebo muffin; mean treatment times were 32 days in the flaxseed group and 39 days in the placebo group. Outcomes were reduced Ki-67 labeling index and c-erbB2 expression, increased apoptosis, and increased urinary lignan excretion in the flaxseed group; adverse events were not verified in the reviewed source. The key limitation reported by the reviewed record is that the treatment period was short and the measured outcomes were tumor biological markers rather than reported clinical outcomes; no additional limitation was verified. [243, 244]\n\nCardiovascular risk markers in hypercholesterolemic adults: double-blind randomized controlled trial in 62 men and postmenopausal women with LDL-C 130–200 mg/dl; 40 g/day ground flaxseed-containing baked products for 10 weeks versus matching wheat-bran products, alongside a low-fat, low-cholesterol diet. Flaxseed reduced LDL-C at 5 weeks but not significantly at 10 weeks, reduced lipoprotein(a) and HOMA-IR at 10 weeks, did not affect the reported inflammation or oxidative-stress markers, and lowered HDL-C in men; the trial reported that flaxseed was well-tolerated. The key limitation reported by the source was that the LDL-C effect was modest and short-lived, and the HDL-C reduction in men warranted additional study. [244, 245]\n\nCardiovascular disease markers in healthy menopausal women: randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial; 199 women assigned to 40 g/day flaxseed or wheat-germ placebo for 12 months, with 179 available for intention-to-treat analysis. Outcomes included increased plasma alpha-linolenic, docosapentaenoic, and total omega-3 fatty acids, between-group differences in apolipoproteins A-1 and B, increased lipoprotein(a) in both groups, and decreased LDL peak particle size in both groups. Adverse events were not verified in the reviewed source. The source reported a limited effect on apolipoprotein metabolism; other key limitations were not verified in the reviewed source. [245]",
    "efficacyScore": 3,
