    "latestVerifiedPublicationYear": 2021,
    "humanStudyCount": 2,
    "totalStudyCount": 3,
    "evidenceByGoal": "Lipid profile / hyperlipoproteinemia — formulation-specific findings:\n• Artichoke dry extract CY450 coated tablets: double-blind, randomized, placebo-controlled, multicenter trial in 143 adults with initial total cholesterol >7.3 mmol/L (>280 mg/dL). [249] The intervention was 1,800 mg/day for 6 weeks versus placebo. [249] Total cholesterol and LDL cholesterol improved significantly versus placebo (p=0.0001); total cholesterol decreased 18.5% versus 8.6% with placebo, and LDL cholesterol decreased 22.9% versus 6.3%. [249] No drug-related adverse events were reported in this study. [249] Key limitation: not verified in reviewed source.\n• Artichoke leaf extract capsule: double-blind, randomized, placebo-controlled trial in 38 CKD subjects with GFR <60 and >15 mL/min/1.73 m² and hypercholesterolemia. [251] Participants received 320 mg twice daily for 6 weeks versus indistinguishable placebo; lipid profile and appetite were assessed at week 8. [251] Total cholesterol and LDL-C decreased significantly versus control (P=0.028 and P=0.005), while HDL-C and triglycerides did not differ significantly. [251] Appetite also differed significantly between groups (P=0.016). [251] Adverse events: not verified in reviewed source. Key limitation: not verified in reviewed source.\nThe 2021 artichoke leaf extract record was not counted as an eligible clinical outcome study because it assessed serum metabolites by incubation with human hepatocytes, chondrocytes, and adipocytes rather than reporting clinical participant outcomes. [250]",
