    "evidenceByGoal": "Weight management/body composition: The 2024 primary clinical investigation studied a novel water-soluble GCB70® extract enriched in 70% total chlorogenic acid and less than 1% caffeine in 105 participants for 12 consecutive weeks; the provided source does not verify the study design, participant sex/age details, comparator, or adverse-event counts. GCB70® was administered at 500 mg twice daily and was associated with approximately 6% lower body weight, approximately 5.65% lower BMI, reduced waist circumference, lower leptin, lower fasting glucose, reduced HbA1c, and reduced TSH; the source reports broad-spectrum safety based on blood chemistry. [270] The 2021 randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled primary clinical study evaluated CGA-7™ in healthy overweight men and women (BMI ≥25 to <30 kg/m²; N=71), with 60 completing the study; participants received 500 mg daily or placebo for 12 weeks. Body weight and BMI were significantly reduced versus placebo, and body fat percentage, fat mass, lean mass, and serum lipid profile changed significantly; the source reports no adverse effects and good tolerability. [271] Key limitations: the records test different extracts rather than one normalized generic formulation; the 2024 summary does not verify a comparator or detailed design, and the 2021 study had 71 randomized and 60 completers, with no further attrition analysis, effect estimates, or detailed adverse-event data in the reviewed summaries. [271]",
    "efficacyScore": 2,
    "evidenceQualityScore": 2,
    "safetySummary": "For GCB70®, the source states that safety was affirmed and that total blood chemistry analysis exhibited broad-spectrum safety, but it does not provide detailed adverse-event counts in the reviewed summary. [270] For CGA-7™, the source reports no adverse effects and that the product was well tolerated during the 12-week study; it also states that blood biochemical and hematological parameters marginally varied in the treatment groups. [271] These findings are limited to the respective short-term studies and formulations.",
    "knownInteractions": "Not mentioned in source. [269, 270, 271]",
