    "latestVerifiedPublicationYear": 2018,
    "humanStudyCount": 2,
    "totalStudyCount": 3,
    "evidenceByGoal": "Weight loss: one small, single-arm pilot study in 5 obese young adults (4 women, 1 man; mean age 20.4 ± 0.9 years; BMI 34.9 ± 3.0 kg/m2) used 200 mg raspberry ketone daily for 6 weeks, with participants instructed to maintain baseline diet and exercise; outcomes were measured at baseline and study end. Mean body weight changed by −0.1 ± 0.3 kg, body fat by 0.0 ± 0.1%, resting energy expenditure increased by 156 ± 47 kcal/day but not significantly, and respiratory quotient changed by +0.01 ± 0.01. The study concluded that raspberry ketone was not an effective weight-loss aid. Comparator: baseline/pre–post comparison; no separate comparator was reported. Adverse events: not verified in reviewed source. Key limitation: very small pilot sample and no separate comparator. [272]\nCYP3A activity: one controlled two-phase human study included 7 healthy women aged 20–35 years. Participants took 3 tablets of 16.7 mg each, three times daily (150.3 mg/day total) for 7 days; urine was sampled after the RK phase and control phase, and the urinary 6β-hydroxycortisol/cortisol ratio was compared. The study reported no significant difference between phases and concluded that RK does not affect CYP3A activity. Comparator: control phase. Adverse events: not verified in reviewed source. Key limitation: very small sample and assessment of a urinary enzyme-activity marker rather than clinical drug-interaction outcomes. [273]\nThe third candidate record was an animal mouse study and therefore was not counted as an eligible human outcome study. [274]",
    "efficacyScore": 2,
    "evidenceQualityScore": 2,
    "safetySummary": "Human-study adverse events were not mentioned in the reviewed source summaries. The excluded animal study reported 67% mortality at 500 mg/kg in mice, while the supplied summary reported no mortality at 330 mg/kg and 165 mg/kg; these animal findings do not establish human safety. [274]",
    "knownInteractions": "In the reviewed 7-person human study, raspberry ketone was assessed for CYP3A activity using the urinary 6β-hydroxycortisol/cortisol ratio; the study reported no significant difference and concluded that RK does not affect CYP3A activity. No other interactions were verified in reviewed sources. [273]",
    "regulatoryStatus": "Not verified in reviewed sources. [272, 273, 274]",
    "limitations": "The audit is restricted to the three supplied records. Two were small human studies and one was an animal mouse study; formulations and study purposes differed, so findings were not transferred across them. Adverse-event reporting was not present in the supplied summaries, and no regulatory or broader interaction conclusions were verified. [272] [273] [274]",
