    "inputName": "Pygeum",
    "canonicalName": "Pygeum africanum extract [277, 278, 279]",
    "aliases": "Pygeum; Pygeum africanum; Pygeum africanum extract; Tadenan (Pygeum africanum extract) [277, 278, 279]",
    "category": "Botanical extract / phytotherapy for benign prostatic hyperplasia–associated lower urinary tract symptoms [277, 278, 279]",
    "latestVerifiedPublicationYear": 1998,
    "humanStudyCount": 2,
    "totalStudyCount": 3,
    "evidenceByGoal": "Goal: benign prostatic hyperplasia–associated micturitional disorders/lower urinary tract symptoms. Barlet et al. (1990): multicentre, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial in 263 patients; Pygeum africanum extract 50 mg per capsule, one capsule morning and evening (100 mg/day), for 60 days; placebo comparator. Outcomes included residual urine, uroflowmetry, diurnal and nocturnal urinary frequency, and subjective symptoms. Micturition improved in 66% of the Pygeum group versus 31% of the placebo group (p<0.001). Adverse events were gastrointestinal side effects in 5 patients, with treatment discontinued in 3; key limitation: not verified in reviewed source. [278]\n\nBreza et al. (1998): open, three-centre efficacy and safety study in men aged 50–75 years meeting specified symptom, quality-of-life, and urinary-flow criteria; 85 patients completed the study. Pygeum africanum extract 50 mg twice daily was given for 2 months, followed by 1 month without treatment; no comparator was reported. Outcomes were IPSS, quality of life, nocturnal frequency, maximum and average urinary flow, post-void residual volume, and prostatic volume. After treatment, IPSS and quality of life improved by 40% and 31%, respectively; nocturnal frequency decreased by 32%; maximum and average urinary flow and urine volume improved significantly, while the post-void residual-volume change was not statistically significant; prostatic volume and sexual quality of life were unchanged. No treatment-related adverse effects were observed. Key limitation: open, non-comparative design. [279]",
    "efficacyScore": 3,
    "evidenceQualityScore": 3,
    "safetySummary": "In the Barlet et al. study, gastrointestinal side effects occurred in 5 patients and treatment was discontinued in 3 of those cases. [278] In the Breza et al. study, no treatment-related adverse effects were observed. [279]",
    "knownInteractions": "Not verified in reviewed sources. [277, 278, 279]",
    "regulatoryStatus": "Not verified in reviewed sources. [277, 278, 279]",
    "limitations": "The 1998 Cochrane record is a systematic review and was not counted as a primary human study. The two eligible primary records provide short-term evidence only; one is open and non-comparative, and the supplied records do not establish longer-term efficacy or comparative effectiveness against standard BPH medicines. Formulation-specific results were not pooled beyond the individually described Pygeum africanum extract preparations. [278] [279]",
    "missingFields": "For the Barlet et al. study, a key limitation was not verified in the reviewed source. For the Breza et al. study, the source did not report a randomized allocation method, and the supplied records did not verify longer-term outcomes, interaction data, or regulatory status. [278] [279]",
