    "evidenceByGoal": "Postmenopausal osteoporosis: 29-month double-blind study in 23 treated and 23 control postmenopausal osteoporotic women; the reported outcomes were total body calcium, regional bone mass, and spinal radiographs. Total body calcium increased 4.4% from baseline in the treated group, with a significant between-group difference, and no new spinal compression fractures occurred in the treated group versus three in controls. Regional bone mass did not differ significantly between groups. The supplied summary does not report the dose or comparator details beyond identifying the control group; adverse events included SGOT elevations or other side effects in 76% of treated subjects, without effects severe enough to stop medication. Key limitation: findings differed by skeletal measure, with no significant regional-bone-mass difference and no significant bone-biopsy difference. [395]\n\nGeriatric clinical evaluation: the record states that the study tested the clinical value of stanozolol in a group of geriatric patients. Sample size, design, dose, duration, comparator, outcomes, adverse events, and key limitation are not verified in the reviewed source summary. [396]\n\nPostphlebitic syndrome: double-blind crossover study in patients with postphlebitic syndrome; the summary reports evidence of benefit and enhanced fibrinolytic activity. Dose was 5 mg twice per day, and no side effects of consequence were noted. Duration, sample size, comparator details, specific clinical outcome measurements, and key limitation are not verified in the reviewed source summary. [397]",
