    "evidenceByGoal": "1) HIV-associated weight loss and body cell mass gain in HIV-infected men: Design: randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial [398]. Population and sample size: 262 HIV-infected men with documented 10% to 20% weight loss or body mass index ≤20 kg/m2 [398]. Dose: placebo or 20, 40, or 80 mg of oxandrolone daily during the randomized phase; subjects could then receive open-label oxandrolone 20 mg for another 12 weeks [398]. Duration: 12-week double-blind phase, followed by an optional 12-week open-label phase [398]. Comparator: placebo [398]. Outcome: body weight increased in all groups; at 12 weeks, only weight gain with 40 mg and body-cell-mass gain with 40 or 80 mg were greater than placebo [398]. Adverse events: treatment was generally well tolerated but was associated with significant increases in transaminases and low-density lipoprotein and decreases in high-density lipoprotein [398]. Key limitation: not verified in reviewed source.\n\n2) Body composition and cardiometabolic health in prepubertal boys with Klinefelter syndrome: Design and population: secondary analysis of a randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled clinical trial in 93 boys with Klinefelter syndrome aged 4 to 12 years. Dose and duration: oral oxandrolone 0.06 mg/kg/day or placebo for 2 years. Comparator: placebo. Outcome: percent body-fat standard-deviation score was significantly lower with oxandrolone than placebo at 2 years; triglycerides were lower, while HDL cholesterol was also lower and bone age advanced more rapidly [400]. Adverse events: not verified in reviewed source; reported safety-related findings were lower HDL cholesterol and more rapid bone-age advancement [400]. Key limitation: the record identifies the analysis as secondary; other key limitations were not verified in reviewed source.",
    "efficacyScore": 3,
