    "catalogId": "oxymetholone",
    "inputName": "Oxymetholone",
    "canonicalName": "Oxymetholone [401, 402, 403]",
    "aliases": "Oxymetholone [401, 402, 403]",
    "category": "Anabolic steroid [401, 402, 403]",
    "latestVerifiedPublicationYear": 2003,
    "humanStudyCount": 2,
    "totalStudyCount": 3,
    "evidenceByGoal": "Clinically studied goal: HIV-associated wasting, including weight gain, body composition, appetite, and quality of life.\n\n• 2003 phase III study: double-blind, randomized, placebo-controlled trial in 89 HIV-positive eugonadal women and men with wasting. Oxymetholone was given at 50 mg twice daily or three times daily for 16 weeks, followed by open-label treatment; placebo was the comparator. Weight gain was 3.0 ± 0.5 kg in the three-times-daily group and 3.5 ± 0.7 kg in the twice-daily group versus 1.0 ± 0.7 kg with placebo. Body cell mass increased by 3.8 ± 0.4 kg in the twice-daily group and 2.1 ± 0.6 kg in the three-times-daily group; appetite, food intake, well-being, and weakness also improved. The most important reported adverse event was liver-associated toxicity. A key limitation was not verified in the reviewed source.\n\n• 1996 prospective pilot study: 30 patients with advanced HIV-1 infection and chronic cachexia were randomly assigned to oxymetholone monotherapy (n=14) or oxymetholone plus ketotifen (n=16), and compared with 30 untreated matched controls, for 30 weeks. Oxymetholone dose was not reported in the reviewed source. Peak weight gain averaged 8.2 kg with monotherapy and 6.1 kg with combination treatment, compared with 1.8 kg weight loss in untreated controls; the Karnofsky index and reported quality-of-life variables also improved. The source reported that oxymetholone was safe but did not provide specific adverse-event data. The key limitation reported by the source was that this was a pilot study and that a randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled multicentre trial was still needed. [401, 402, 403]",
    "efficacyScore": 2,
    "evidenceQualityScore": 2,
    "safetySummary": "Liver-associated toxicity was the most important adverse event reported in the 2003 phase III record. One record reported alanine aminotransferase increases greater than five times baseline in 35% of the three-times-daily group, 27% of the twice-daily group, and 0% of the placebo group; the other apparently duplicate record reported greater-than-five-times-baseline increases in ALT, AST, or gamma-GT in 43%, 25%, and 8% of the corresponding groups. The duplicate records therefore report discrepant toxicity percentages. The 1996 pilot stated that oxymetholone was safe but did not provide specific adverse-event data. [401, 402, 403]",
    "knownInteractions": "The reviewed pilot studied oxymetholone both alone and with ketotifen; it reported that adding ketotifen did not further support weight gain. No broader interaction assessment was reported in the reviewed sources. [401, 402, 403]",
    "regulatoryStatus": "Not verified in reviewed sources. [401, 402, 403]",
    "limitations": "The three candidate records include two apparently duplicate reports of the same 2003 phase III trial; therefore, only two uniquely identifiable primary human studies were counted. Evidence is limited to HIV-associated wasting. The 1996 study is explicitly described as a prospective pilot and called for a larger randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled multicentre trial. The reviewed sources do not verify broader indications or a key limitation for the 2003 trial. [401, 402, 403]",
    "missingFields": "Oxymetholone dose was not reported in the reviewed source for the 1996 pilot; specific adverse-event details for that pilot and a key limitation for the 2003 trial were not verified in the reviewed sources. [401, 402, 403]",
