    "evidenceByGoal": "Goal: advanced, metastatic, or disseminated breast carcinoma, including painful bone metastases. Study 1 was a case report in a 55-year-old premenopausal woman with painful bone metastases; sample size was one patient. The formulation was drostanolone propionate, 100 mg intramuscularly every week, started in December 1967. Pain had completely disappeared within two weeks and recurred after five months. Comparator: not verified in reviewed source. Adverse events: not verified in reviewed source. Key limitation: single-patient case report. Study 2 was a randomized clinical study in metastatic breast cancer evaluating drostanolone-containing hormone and chemotherapy applications; the supplied record does not verify the sample size or dose. The observation period was 10 to 16 weeks. The reported outcome was objective response: single-hormone therapy with drostanolone and the stated comparators had similar objective-response rates, while drostanolone plus cyclophosphamide was less beneficial than testosterone decanoate or testolactone plus cyclophosphamide in the supplied summary; combination therapy increased objective-response rates from 22–25% to 46–55%. Adverse events: not verified in reviewed source. Key limitation: sample size, dose, adverse-event data, and detailed methods were not verified in the supplied record. Record 3 states treatment intent for disseminated breast carcinoma but provides no verified primary human outcome details in the supplied summary. [413, 414, 415]",
