    "catalogId": "mesterolone",
    "inputName": "Mesterolone",
    "canonicalName": "Mesterolone [417, 418, 419]",
    "aliases": "Mesterolone; 1 alpha-methyl-5-alpha-androstane-17 beta-ol-3-one [417, 418, 419]",
    "category": "Not mentioned in source. [417, 418, 419]",
    "latestVerifiedPublicationYear": 2014,
    "humanStudyCount": 3,
    "totalStudyCount": 3,
    "evidenceByGoal": "Male infertility/semen quality: (1) A double-blind, placebo-controlled trial included 52 men with idiopathic oligoasthenospermia and/or teratozoospermia; mesterolone was given at 150 mg/day for 12 months. The comparator was placebo. Pregnancy rates were 26% with mesterolone versus 48% with placebo; motility and the proportion of morphologically normal sperm increased with mesterolone, but similar semen improvement also occurred with placebo, leading the authors to doubt its usefulness. Adverse events and a more specific key limitation were not verified in the reviewed source. [417]\n\n(2) A 1975 report described mesterolone in 141 patients with primary or secondary male sterility, including oligospermia, asthenospermia, abnormal sperm viscosity, or limited sperm survival time. The reported dose was 30–75 mg/day for 60 days; comparator and study design were not verified in the reviewed source. At least one symptom improved in 44 patients, and the spouse became pregnant in 19 cases. Side effects were observed in 2 patients; a key limitation was not verified in the reviewed source. [419]\n\nAging male syndrome/lower urinary tract symptoms: A cross-sectional study of 34 patients with aging male syndrome symptoms and/or low testosterone administered oral mesterolone 50 mg/day for 2 months. Assessments were performed before and after treatment; an independent comparator was not verified in the reviewed source. Aging Male Symptoms, International Prostate Symptom Score, and prostate-related quality-of-life scores significantly improved. Prostate-volume change was not statistically significant, total testosterone, sex-hormone-binding globulin, and estradiol decreased, free testosterone did not change, the free-testosterone fraction increased, and total and free PSA did not change. Specific adverse events were not reported in the reviewed source; the report concluded that mesterolone was a safe alternative. The cross-sectional, pre/post design is the key reported design limitation. [418]",
    "efficacyScore": 2,
    "evidenceQualityScore": 2,
    "safetySummary": "The 1975 infertility report stated that mesterolone tolerance was excellent and that side effects occurred in 2 patients. [419] The aging-male-syndrome report concluded that mesterolone was a safe alternative, but specific adverse-event details were not reported in the reviewed summary. [418]",
