    "catalogId": "exemestane",
    "inputName": "Exemestane",
    "canonicalName": "Exemestane [438, 439, 440]",
    "aliases": "Exemestane; EXE; steroidal aromatase inhibitor; oral aromatase inactivator [438, 439, 440]",
    "category": "Aromatase inhibitor; oncology [438, 439, 440]",
    "latestVerifiedPublicationYear": 2009,
    "humanStudyCount": 3,
    "totalStudyCount": 3,
    "evidenceByGoal": "Metastatic/hormone-responsive breast cancer in postmenopausal women: (1) Phase II, multicentre, open-label study of oral exemestane 25 mg daily in postmenopausal women with metastatic breast cancer after tamoxifen failure; 137 patients received exemestane. The reported outcomes were 4 complete responses, 28 partial responses, a 23% overall response rate, and 33 additional patients with stable disease for at least 24 weeks, producing a 47% overall success rate. Median response duration was 69.4 weeks, median time to progression was 25.1 weeks, and median time to treatment failure was 24 weeks. The source reports hot flushes (14%), dizziness (9%), nausea (8%), and increased sweating (5%) as the most common drug-related or indeterminate adverse events. [438] Key limitations: open-label, non-comparative design; the supplied source does not report a separate comparator or a more detailed limitation statement. [438]\n\n(2) Phase III randomized, double-blind, multicentre study in 769 postmenopausal women with metastatic breast cancer after tamoxifen failure: exemestane 25 mg once daily (n=366) was compared with megestrol acetate 160 mg daily (n=403). Exemestane produced complete response plus partial response rates of 15.0% versus 12.4%, complete response plus partial response plus stable disease for at least 24 weeks of 37.4% versus 34.6%, median duration of that disease-control endpoint of 60.1 versus 49.1 weeks, median time to tumor progression of 20.3 versus 16.6 weeks, median time to treatment failure of 16.3 versus 15.7 weeks, and overall survival not reached versus 123.4 weeks; the response-rate and disease-control-rate differences were not statistically significant, whereas the listed time-to-event and survival differences were statistically significant. Both treatments were described as well tolerated; grade 3 or 4 weight gain was 8% with exemestane versus 17% with megestrol acetate. [438] Key limitation: the supplied record is a summary and does not report all adverse events, duration of treatment, or detailed subgroup/population characteristics beyond postmenopausal status and prior tamoxifen failure. [438]\n\n(3) Combination treatment/pharmacokinetics in hormone-responsive advanced breast cancer: phase II study of exemestane 25 mg daily plus fulvestrant 250 mg intramuscularly every 28 days in postmenopausal women; 19 women were enrolled, although the planned sample size was 40. The primary endpoint was the proportion free of progressive disease at 6 months; 9 of 19 women (47%) had progression-free survival of at least 6 months, 8 (42%) progressed before 6 months, and 2 (11%) remained on treatment at the reported cutoff. Exemestane pharmacokinetics were assessed alone on day 7 and with fulvestrant on day 120. [438] Key limitations: small enrollment, incomplete reported follow-up/results, no comparator arm described in the supplied summary, and the prespecified threshold of 24 of 40 women progression-free at 6 months was not reached by the reported 9 of 19 result. [438]",
    "efficacyScore": 4,
    "evidenceQualityScore": 3,
    "safetySummary": "In the open-label metastatic-breast-cancer study, the most common drug-related or indeterminate adverse events were hot flushes (14%), dizziness (9%), nausea (8%), and increased sweating (5%). [438] In the randomized phase III study, both treatments were described as well tolerated; grade 3 or 4 weight gain was reported in 8% of exemestane-treated patients and 17% of megestrol-acetate-treated patients. [440] A complete safety profile, including all adverse events and serious adverse events, is not verified in the reviewed summaries. [439]",
    "knownInteractions": "The reviewed combination study assessed exemestane pharmacokinetics when administered alone and with fulvestrant; a definitive interaction conclusion is not verified in the supplied source text. [439]",
