    "evidenceByGoal": "Endocrine estrogen suppression in postmenopausal breast cancer: multicentre randomized dose-comparison study in 152 postmenopausal breast-cancer patients at first relapse; 73 received formestane 250 mg and 79 received 500 mg intramuscularly every two weeks. [441] Blood sampling occurred on day 1, at 4 and 10 weeks, and every 12 weeks thereafter; tumor response was first assessed after 2.5 months and then every three months. [441] After four weeks, estrone, estradiol, and estrone sulphate were significantly suppressed in both dose groups (P < 0.001). [441] Comparator: the two formestane dose groups. [441] Adverse events: not verified in reviewed source. Key limitation: detailed safety reporting and treatment-duration details beyond the reported assessment schedule were not verified in reviewed source.\n\nAntitumor efficacy in advanced or relapsed postmenopausal breast cancer: the 152-patient randomized dose study reported overall response rates of 30% with 250 mg and 40% with 500 mg. [441] A separate open, comparative randomized multicentre trial enrolled 547 patients with histologically proven advanced breast cancer and compared formestane 250 mg intramuscularly fortnightly with megestrol acetate 160 mg orally once daily. [443] Its primary endpoint was time to treatment failure; secondary endpoints were objective tumor response, time to progression, and overall survival. [443] Numerical efficacy outcomes from that 547-patient trial: not verified in reviewed source. Duration for that trial: not verified in reviewed source. Preliminary safety reporting stated that more serious adverse events, including pulmonary embolism and vaginal bleeding, tended to occur in the megestrol acetate group compared with the LENTARON group; formestane-arm adverse-event details were not verified in reviewed source. [443] Key limitation: the reviewed record reports trial endpoints and preliminary safety language but does not provide the trial's numerical efficacy results or treatment duration.",
