    "humanStudyCount": 1,
    "totalStudyCount": 3,
    "evidenceByGoal": "Goal: endocrine/growth-hormone secretagogue effects. One eligible primary human study used a comparative endocrine challenge design in six young adults; participants received alexamorelin (ALEX) at 1.0 or 2.0 microgram/kg intravenously, or 20 mg orally (approximately 300 microgram/kg), with hexarelin (HEX) as the comparator for the reported comparisons. [462] Duration: not verified in reviewed source. ALEX and HEX produced dose-dependent increases in GH and prolactin; both increased ACTH and cortisol, with the highest ALEX dose producing significantly higher ACTH and cortisol responses than HEX; intravenous ALEX also increased aldosterone, while the oral GH response was higher than HEX but not significantly so. [462] Adverse events: not verified in reviewed source. Key limitation: the study included only six young adults, and therapeutic clinical outcomes were not reported in the reviewed source. Candidate record 2 was anamorelin rather than alexamorelin, and candidate record 3 was an in-vitro pooled-human-hepatocyte metabolism study rather than a primary human outcome study. [460, 463]",
