    "evidenceByGoal": "Endometriosis treatment: In a study of 11 women with laparoscopically confirmed mild or moderate endometriosis, oral gestrinone 2.5 mg twice weekly for 6 months was used; the mean laparoscopic score decreased from 17.18 to 9.09, and painful symptoms were relieved in all patients within 2 months. [513] Design, comparator, adverse events, and a key limitation were not verified in the reviewed source summary. In a separate randomized double-blind study, 20 premenopausal women with laparoscopically confirmed endometriosis received either 1.25 mg or 2.5 mg twice weekly for 6 months. Median total endometriosis scores decreased from 7.5 to 1.0 in the 1.25-mg group and from 7.0 to 0 in the 2.5-mg group, with no significant between-group difference. [514] Comparator: the two active gestrinone regimens; a placebo or untreated comparator was not verified in the reviewed source. Adverse events: not verified in reviewed source. Key limitation: the reviewed human studies were small (11 and 20 participants) and lasted 6 months; longer-term outcomes were not verified in the reviewed sources. [513, 514]\n\nMetabolic and bone outcomes during endometriosis treatment: In the randomized double-blind study, bone density in the spine and proximal femur was conserved at both doses, while low-density lipoprotein concentrations rose by 13% and high-density lipoprotein concentrations fell by 40%. [514] Adverse events beyond these reported lipid changes, and longer-term clinical consequences, were not verified in the reviewed source.",
