    "evidenceByGoal": "Bone mineral density, bone metabolism, and body composition: eligible study design and population were a randomized, placebo-controlled trial in 225 healthy adults aged 55–85 years; the intervention was 50 mg daily oral DHEA for one year versus placebo [556]. DHEA increased serum DHEA and DHEA sulfate levels [556]. In women, testosterone, estradiol, and IGF-1 increased, while these changes were not observed in men [556]; the bone-resorption marker C-terminal telopeptide decreased in women, while bone-specific alkaline phosphatase did not significantly change in either sex [556]. Lumbar-spine BMD improved in women, but hip, femoral-neck, and total-body BMD did not, and no BMD site improved in men [556]. Body composition was not affected in either sex [556]. Adverse events: not verified in reviewed source. Key limitation: not verified in reviewed source.",
