    "evidenceByGoal": "Goal: diagnostic assessment of adrenal androgen excess. Design: comparative clinical biomarker study in women with anovulatory hyperandrogenism, comparing 11β-hydroxyandrostenedione with dehydroepiandrosterone sulfate and assessing responses to stimulation and suppression. [557] Population and sample: 81 women with anovulatory hyperandrogenism and 26 normal women; stimulation/suppression subgroups included n=16, n=8, and n=10 as reported. [557] Dose and duration: 0.25 mg intravenous corticotropin; 100 micrograms intravenous corticotropin-releasing factor; 2 mg dexamethasone for 3 days; and 0.5 mg dexamethasone for 3 months. [557] Comparator: dehydroepiandrosterone sulfate, with additional androgen and cortisol comparisons. [557] Outcome: 65% had elevated 11β-hydroxyandrostenedione versus 57% with elevated dehydroepiandrosterone sulfate; 11β-hydroxyandrostenedione increased by 157% ± 53% after corticotropin, increased by 148% ± 26% after corticotropin-releasing factor, and was suppressed by 95% ± 2% after 2 mg dexamethasone; the authors reported calculated sensitivity and specificity greater than those of dehydroepiandrosterone sulfate. [557] Adverse events: not verified in reviewed source. Key limitation: this record verifies a diagnostic biomarker study, not treatment efficacy, and patient-centered clinical outcomes were not reported in the supplied summary.",
