    "evidenceByGoal": "Emergency contraception — Design: double-blind randomized trial. [588] Population: 1,998 women at 21 worldwide centres with regular menses, not using hormonal contraception, and requesting emergency contraception after one unprotected coitus. [588] Sample size: 1,998 enrolled; pregnancy outcomes were available for 1,955 women, including 976 in the levonorgestrel group and 979 in the Yuzpe group. [588] Dose: levonorgestrel 0.75 mg, repeated 12 hours later. [588] Duration/timing: treatment was started within 72 hours of unprotected coitus, with the second dose 12 hours later. [588] Comparator: Yuzpe regimen containing ethinylestradiol 100 microg plus levonorgestrel 0.5 mg, repeated 12 hours later. [588] Outcome: pregnancy was 1.1% (11/976) with levonorgestrel versus 3.2% (31/979) with Yuzpe; crude relative risk was 0.36 (95% CI 0.18–0.70). [588] Adverse events: nausea was 23.1% versus 50.5%, and vomiting was 5.6% versus 18.8%, significantly less frequent with levonorgestrel than with Yuzpe (p<0.01). [588] Key limitation: outcome was unknown for 43 women, and efficacy of both treatments declined with increasing time since unprotected coitus. [588]",
