    "latestVerifiedPublicationYear": 1992,
    "humanStudyCount": 3,
    "totalStudyCount": 3,
    "evidenceByGoal": "Contraception and gynecologic effects: The reviewed record reports 142 patients receiving lynestrenol alone at 5 mg daily from day 5 of the cycle for a total of 1,382 months, and 75 patients receiving lynestrenol combined with mestranol 0.15 mg from day 5 to day 24 for 727 cycles, for pregnancy protection or gynecologic disorders including infertility. [591] The reported assessments were endometrial studies, cervical mucus, vaginal cytology, and urinary pregnanediol excretion in 27 regularly menstruating patients. [591] Lynestrenol alone was reported to induce hypoestrogenic amenorrhea with sterility, inhibit corpus luteum formation, and produce absence of normal endometrium during treatment; normal endometrial growth recurred several months after stopping treatment. [591] The combined regimen induced sterility with regular menstruation and cyclical endometrial changes, but the summary does not verify a comparator or allocation method. [591] Breakthrough bleeding was the most troublesome side effect, and side effects were reported more frequently with combined treatment. [591] Duration is reported as aggregate months/cycles rather than a uniform participant-level follow-up, and the summary does not verify arm-specific sample sizes, a comparator, or detailed clinical pregnancy outcomes.\n\nPostmenopausal hot flushes: This was a parallel randomized placebo-controlled clinical trial of 42 women with menopausal flushes after natural or artificial menopause. [591] The trial lasted six weeks; lynestrenol began at 10 mg/day and declined to 1.25 mg/day, while placebo was the comparator. [591] Lynestrenol improved patients' subjective condition, reduced gonadotropin production significantly better than placebo, and produced subjective relief after the second week; the summary reports that effects were equal to placebo-superior treatment effects at the relevant comparison after six weeks. [591] No serious undesirable effects, liver-function or serum-lipid changes, or excess endometrial bleeding versus placebo were recorded. [591] The summary does not verify arm-specific sample sizes, numerical effect estimates, or a detailed adverse-event breakdown.\n\nHuman uterine contractility: This was a longitudinal before-and-after study in 10 patients requesting fertility control; all studies were longitudinal. [591] Lynestrenol was administered orally at 0.5 mg beginning on the first day of menstruation and continuously for several cycles; contractility tests lasted 2–3 hours and were performed before and after treatment at 5–7-day intervals during both cycle phases. [592] The reported outcome was increased tone, intensity, and frequency of nonpregnant uterine contractions, with more marked effects during the estrogenic phase. [592] A concurrent comparator and adverse events were not verified in the reviewed summary; the main limitation is the small longitudinal pre/post design with short test sessions and no reported randomized control.",
    "efficacyScore": 2,
    "evidenceQualityScore": 2,
    "safetySummary": "Breakthrough bleeding was reported as the most troublesome side effect with lynestrenol alone, and side effects were more frequent with combined treatment. [591] In the postmenopausal flush trial, no serious undesirable effects, liver-function changes, or serum-lipid changes were recorded, and endometrial bleeding was not more frequent than with placebo. [591] Adverse events were not verified in the uterine-contractility study summary. No broader safety conclusion is verified from these records.",
