    "latestVerifiedPublicationYear": 1972,
    "humanStudyCount": 2,
    "totalStudyCount": 3,
    "evidenceByGoal": "Contraception — progestogen-only regimen: RCT record; 144 women of proved fertility. Norgestrel was given continually at 75 μg/day. The report gives outcomes through the first 12 and 30 cycles and references completion of 30 months on trial. Comparator: not verified in reviewed source. Contraceptive outcome: failure rate 2.1% within the first 12 cycles and 3.6% within the first 30 cycles by the life-table method; the Pearl-index estimate was 2.0 conceptions per 100 woman-years. Reported adverse findings included irregular and generally short bleeding intervals, with about one-fifth of cycles lasting less than 17 days; no confirmed thromboembolism was observed. Key limitation: 57 of 144 women withdrew for method-related reasons before completing 30 months, with more than half of those withdrawals attributed to irregular menstruation. [594]\n\nContraception — combined regimen: separate RCT record; 58 patients received norgestrel 0.5 mg plus ethinyl estradiol 0.05 mg for 21 days per month for 487 cycles. Comparator: not verified in reviewed source. Outcome: no pregnancies occurred, and 3.2% of cycles had spotting or breakthrough bleeding. In the reported subgroup of 47 patients using 0.5 mg norgestrel plus 0.05 mg ethinyl estradiol for oral contraception or amenorrhea, reported adverse-event frequencies included nausea 8.4%, tiredness 6.2%, headache 5.2%, breast tenderness 3.6%, depression/irritability 3.2%, bloated feeling 0.7%, abdominal discomfort 0.5%, vomiting 0.4%, edema 0.3%, and blurred vision 0.1%. This combined regimen is kept separate from the progestogen-only regimen and is not pooled with it.\n\nAmenorrhea/cycle control: in the separate clinical-trial record, 9 patients with primary amenorrhea and 10 with secondary amenorrhea were treated for 300 cycles. Satisfactory induction of withdrawal bleeding and cycle control was reported when norgestrel 0.5 mg was given with ethinyl estradiol 0.05 mg for 21 days per month. Comparator, duration beyond the reported 300 cycles, and goal-specific adverse events: not verified in reviewed source. Key limitation: not verified in reviewed source.\n\nMenometrorrhagia: norgestrel alone did not control menometrorrhagia at doses up to 4 mg; when ethinyl estradiol 0.05 mg was added and the dose was given four times a day, satisfactory suppression of bleeding was reported. Population, duration, comparator, and adverse events for this goal: not verified in reviewed source. Key limitation: not verified in reviewed source.",
    "efficacyScore": 2,
    "evidenceQualityScore": 2,
    "safetySummary": "Source-reported adverse findings only: the progestogen-only trial reported irregular and generally short bleeding intervals, about one-fifth of cycles shorter than 17 days, and no confirmed thromboembolism; 57 of 144 participants withdrew for method-related reasons before completing 30 months, with more than half of those withdrawals attributed to irregular menstruation. [594] The separate combined-regimen record reported the listed rates of nausea, tiredness, headache, breast tenderness, depression/irritability, bloated feeling, abdominal discomfort, vomiting, edema, and blurred vision. Overall safety beyond these reported findings: not verified in reviewed sources.",
