    "evidenceByGoal": "Contraceptive effectiveness and acceptability: only the record explicitly concerning norgestrienone was eligible; the other two records concern norgestimate and were identity mismatches. Design: not verified in reviewed source. Population/sample size: 145 women. [598] Dose/formulation: subdermal implants delivering approximately 225 microgram/day. [598] Duration/exposure: 2,259 woman-months of use; the source concluded implants should be replaced every 12 months. [598] Comparator: not mentioned in source. Outcome: five pregnancies occurred, and the 12-month continuation rate was 86.7%; bleeding-pattern changes led to discontinuation in four cases. [598] Reported adverse events/findings included increased bleeding runs and bleeding days in approximately 12% of subjects, no bleeding in the first 90 days in 10%, headache, and signs of mild androgenicity. [598] Key limitation: comparator and detailed study-design information were not reported in the reviewed source, and the evidence is from one candidate record.",
    "efficacyScore": 2,
    "evidenceQualityScore": 2,
    "safetySummary": "The eligible source reports increased bleeding runs and bleeding days in approximately 12% of subjects, no bleeding in the first 90 days in 10%, headache, and signs of mild androgenicity. [598] It also reports normal values for 17 blood and urine parameters, a tendency toward lower cholesterolemia in several patients, and one subject with cortisol slightly below the lower normal range without clinical manifestations of hypoadrenalism. [598] These are reported findings from the reviewed study, not a broader safety conclusion.",
