    "evidenceByGoal": "Testosterone restoration in men with secondary hypogonadism: one record's stated objective was to assess daily oral enclomiphene citrate versus topical testosterone gel for serum total testosterone, LH, FSH, and sperm counts in men with secondary hypogonadism; design, population details beyond men with secondary hypogonadism, sample size, dose, duration, comparator details, adverse events, and key limitation were not verified in the reviewed source. Obese hypogonadal men: the record title reports oral enclomiphene citrate raising testosterone and preserving sperm counts compared with topical testosterone, but design, sample size, dose, duration, adverse events, and key limitation were not verified in the reviewed source. Testosterone increase in men with low testosterone: a randomized study enrolled 52 subjects and assigned oral enclomiphene citrate at 12.5 mg, 25 mg, or 50 mg, topical testosterone gel at 5 g, or oral placebo daily for 14 days, with follow-up 7–10 days after the final dose. The reported outcome was a significant dose-dependent rise in total testosterone across all enclomiphene doses and topical testosterone, but not placebo; supraphysiologic total-testosterone levels were observed in topical-testosterone-treated subjects. Adverse-event findings and a source-reported key limitation were not verified in the reviewed source. [631, 632, 633]",
