    "evidenceByGoal": "Strength and lean tissue accrual: randomized pilot study in 16 resistance-trained men, with 7 receiving phosphatidic acid and 9 receiving placebo. [649] The intervention was 750 mg of phosphatidic acid daily, combined with resistance training 4 days per week for 8 weeks, versus placebo. [649] Outcomes included 1-RM bench press and squat strength, body composition, and vastus lateralis muscle thickness and pennation angle. [649] The phosphatidic-acid group had a 12.7% increase in squat strength and a 2.6% increase in lean body mass; the placebo group had a 9.3% increase in squat strength and a 0.1% change in lean body mass. [649] The authors characterized the findings as a likely benefit for lower-body strength and a very likely benefit for lean body mass, but parametric analysis was unable to demonstrate significant differences. [649] Adverse events: not verified in reviewed source. Key limitation: pilot study with a small sample and no significant differences demonstrated by parametric analysis. [649]",
