    "humanStudyCount": 2,
    "totalStudyCount": 3,
    "evidenceByGoal": "Eligible unique study 1 — urinary eicosanoid synthesis: randomized/crossover dietary intervention in 10 healthy men living in a metabolic unit. The low-arachidonic-acid comparator diet contained 210 mg/day for 65 days; the high-arachidonic-acid diet added 1.5 g/day for 50 days. [651] The reported outcomes were increased urinary 11-dehydrothromboxane B2 excretion, including a 41% increase from low-AA to high-AA, and increased PGI2-M excretion. [651] Adverse events: not verified in reviewed source. Key limitation: not reported in reviewed source.\n\nEligible unique study 2 — serum fatty-acid composition and platelet aggregation: double-blind, placebo-controlled study in 24 healthy Japanese men. [653] The arachidonate-enriched triglyceride intervention supplied 838 mg arachidonic acid/day for 2 weeks; the reported serum-phospholipid increase was maintained for 4 weeks and returned to baseline after a 4-week washout. [653] The comparator was placebo; background dietary arachidonic-acid intake was 175 mg/day in the arachidonic-acid group and 179 mg/day in the placebo group. [653] Outcomes included increased arachidonic acid in serum phospholipids and triacylglycerol, decreased linoleic acid in serum phospholipids, no change in EPA or DHA, and no change in platelet aggregation, physical characteristics, or biochemical parameters. [653] Adverse events: not verified in reviewed source. Key limitation: not reported in reviewed source.\n\nRecord 2 was treated as a design/rationale companion rather than a separate eligible outcome study: it describes a 130-day controlled crossover study and states that its results would be presented in subsequent papers. [652]",
