    "latestVerifiedPublicationYear": 2026,
    "humanStudyCount": 2,
    "totalStudyCount": 3,
    "evidenceByGoal": "Metabolic outcomes in adults with diabetes: a pilot clinical study enrolled 44 diabetic patients from Adrar city. The source reports 3,600 mg of Moringa oleifera leaf powder twice daily, at breakfast and 7 p.m., for 90 consecutive days, alongside regular hypoglycemic medications; outcomes were assessed on days 0, 3, 7, 30, and 90. [657] The study evaluated serum lipids, blood sugar, HbA1c, weight, BMI, and blood pressure. [657] It reported statistically significant effects on blood sugar/HbA1c, LDL-C, and HDL-C, with LDL-C and HbA1c reduced and HDL-C elevated; weight, BMI, and blood pressure were not significantly affected. [657] No side effect was reported. [657] Comparator and randomization were not verified in the reviewed source. The key limitation was its preliminary pilot nature; the source states that a more extensive trial is needed to determine the optimal dose and long-term advantages. [657]\n\nModerate acute malnutrition in children: a randomized double-blind clinical trial enrolled 400 children aged 6–59 months with moderate acute malnutrition at outpatient nutritional recovery centers. The intervention was Moringa oleifera dry leaf powder plus ready-to-use supplementary foods (RUSF), compared with RUSF plus placebo. [657] The source does not report the dose or exact supplementation duration; these fields are not verified in the reviewed source. There was no difference in average weight gain, mid-upper-arm circumference, or height. Median length of stay was 5 weeks with Moringa and 4 weeks with placebo, with no statistically significant difference (P=0.522); the cure rate was 82% in the Moringa group, with RR 1.03 (0.94 to 1.13). Renal and hepatic toxicity was not observed. The key limitation reported by the source is that supplementation in children already receiving dietary treatment, with the dose reduced to a minimum and the supplementation duration, may have contributed to the absence of effect.",
    "efficacyScore": 2,
    "evidenceQualityScore": 2,
    "safetySummary": "In the diabetes pilot, no side effect was reported by any participant. [657] In the pediatric malnutrition trial, renal and hepatic toxicity was not observed. These findings are limited to the reported trial observations and do not establish broader safety across formulations, doses, durations, or populations.",
